  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "The gate passed: **OK**, with 7 witnesses, on the third of the 6 allowed runs.\n\n**Rule** (`LVMC_11_30_140_B_o1.lean`):\n- **Who it applies to:** a pedestrian who isn't of the white-cane / guided class, or any vehicle.\n- **When it applies:** some other road user of the white-cane class is present, and either contact with them begins, or the centre distance first falls to `p.blind_person_approach_reach` while the subject is moving.\n- **Duty:** the subject comes to a full stop (`drv_motion_complete_halt`) at some point in the `p.immediate_stop_window` ahead.\n- **Narrowings:** none; the node's record decided every reading.\n\n**Witnesses:**\n- All of them judge t = 1–9 s, because \"contact begins\" and \"distance first falls\" need a previous observation, which t = 0 doesn't have.\n- I added `branch_3` (not applicable): a car already at rest when a white-cane pedestrian walks up to it. It's there so the \"while moving\" condition is actually tested.\n\n**Gate runs:**\n1. **WITNESS_FAIL:** `blind` came out violated, not unknown. Masking `rel.centre_distance` in the manifest doesn't hide it, because Core recalculates the distance from the two positions. I left the protected pedestrian's position unobserved and stated `contact := false`, which Core can't work out without that position.\n2. **WITNESS_FAIL:** the adapter rejected that trace because track 2 was marked as having observed occupancy but had no footprint. I set `occupancyObserved := [1]`.\n3. **OK.**\n\n**For the harness/planner:** masking `rel.centre_distance` alone can't make it unknown while both positions are observed, so a `blind` witness for this atom also has to leave a position unobserved. This is noted in `task_result.md`.\n\nOne `grep` of Core to check how `rises` treats the first observation was refused, since only the gate command may run. Setting `window` to skip t = 0 made that check unnecessary.",
